    outlook: "The immediate future for RNA research involves accelerated clinical trials for oncology, autoimmune diseases, and rare genetic disorders. We can anticipate new regulatory approvals for RNA-based drugs in the United States, United Kingdom, Canada, and Australia within the next 3-5 years. Further advancements will focus on delivery mechanisms to ensure RNA reaches target cells effectively and safely. For robotic surgery in India, the NIMS Hyderabad model is likely to be replicated across other public and private institutions, driven by government incentives and increasing awareness. We expect to see a diversification of robotic platforms, potentially including more specialized or domestically developed systems. The integration of artificial intelligence and machine learning will further enhance the precision and autonomy of robotic surgeries, making procedures even safer and more efficient.",
    conclusion: "The twin narratives of cutting-edge RNA research promising a new era of medical cures and India's remarkable achievement in making advanced robotic surgery accessible are powerful indicators of global healthcare's trajectory. These developments are not just isolated scientific or technological feats; they represent a fundamental shift towards precision, accessibility, and transformative patient care. As RNA therapeutics move from labs to clinics worldwide and as India continues to democratize high-tech surgery, the collective impact will redefine what's possible in health, offering hope and improved outcomes for millions across the globe.",
